WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.160
Both sides of your heart pump the same flow of blood every minute.

2
00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:11.120
So why does the left side push so much harder than the right?

3
00:00:12.183 --> 00:00:17.063
Start with flow. Flow is the volume of blood moved each minute.

4
00:00:18.303 --> 00:00:23.163
One ventricle's flow is its heart rate times its stroke volume, the blood it ejects

5
00:00:23.163 --> 00:00:24.163
with each beat.

6
00:00:24.983 --> 00:00:26.323
Now follow the loop.

7
00:00:27.623 --> 00:00:30.743
The right ventricle sends oxygen-poor blood through the lungs.

8
00:00:32.083 --> 00:00:36.063
It comes back oxygen-rich to the left side, which sends it through the body.

9
00:00:37.167 --> 00:00:40.247
The two sides pump one after the other, in series.

10
00:00:41.767 --> 00:00:45.547
Blood leaving one side must pass through the other before it comes back.

11
00:00:46.707 --> 00:00:49.587
So over time, their average flows must be equal.

12
00:00:50.667 --> 00:00:52.267
Now draw pressure as height.

13
00:00:53.587 --> 00:00:56.207
Each ventricle is a lift that raises blood up.

14
00:00:57.287 --> 00:01:01.147
Then blood rolls downhill through its circuit, from high pressure to low.

15
00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:04.380
Some circuits are harder to push through.

16
00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:08.540
Narrow vessels resist the flow, like a narrow pipe.

17
00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:10.800
Call that resistance.

18
00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:16.180
The pressure drop across a circuit equals the flow times its resistance.

19
00:01:17.350 --> 00:01:19.690
The lungs resist the flow only a little.

20
00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:25.330
So the right ventricle needs only a small lift to drive the whole flow through them.

21
00:01:26.333 --> 00:01:28.573
The body's circuit resists far more.

22
00:01:29.673 --> 00:01:34.013
So the left ventricle must lift the same flow much higher, to drive it down that

23
00:01:34.013 --> 00:01:35.413
long, narrow slope.

24
00:01:36.467 --> 00:01:38.227
Same flow, different costs.

25
00:01:39.547 --> 00:01:44.247
At the same flow, the circuit with more resistance needs the bigger pressure drop.

26
00:01:45.507 --> 00:01:48.207
Two separate pumps let each circuit get its own pressure.

27
00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:54.760
One caution. This rule is bookkeeping on averages over many beats.

28
00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:59.420
In a living body, the heart and the vessels set the flow together.

29
00:02:00.533 --> 00:02:02.073
Now a heart with one pump.

30
00:02:03.133 --> 00:02:06.433
A teleost fish, such as a trout, has one ventricle.

31
00:02:07.593 --> 00:02:12.113
It drives blood through its narrow gill capillaries first, then on to the body.

32
00:02:13.217 --> 00:02:14.497
Here is the question.

33
00:02:15.937 --> 00:02:18.037
Blood leaves the gills and reaches the body.

34
00:02:19.277 --> 00:02:22.817
Is its pressure the same as the ventricle made, higher, or lower?

35
00:02:29.383 --> 00:02:35.643
Lower. The gill capillaries resist the flow, so part of the pressure is spent there.

36
00:02:36.843 --> 00:02:39.183
The body is supplied with only what is left.

37
00:02:40.267 --> 00:02:42.487
Birds and mammals avoid that trade.

38
00:02:43.787 --> 00:02:44.787
They have two pumps.

39
00:02:46.007 --> 00:02:49.187
The lungs run at low pressure and the body at high pressure.

40
00:02:50.447 --> 00:02:51.707
Both carry the full flow.

41
00:02:52.767 --> 00:02:54.387
One flow, two costs.

42
00:02:55.627 --> 00:02:57.147
Both sides move the same blood.

43
00:02:58.287 --> 00:03:01.567
The harder circuit, the body's, takes the bigger pressure drop.
